1
00:00:00,059 --> 00:00:03,480
Pending calls let dispatchers prepare a
record before releasing

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:05,010
it to selected responders.

3
00:00:05,389 --> 00:00:08,699
This walkthrough uses one clearly marked
software exercise

4
00:00:08,699 --> 00:00:09,899
in a fictional department.

5
00:00:10,449 --> 00:00:12,579
Open Calls and choose New Call.

6
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:18,360
Enter a recognizable Name and a concise
Nature, then review Priority and Type.

7
00:00:18,809 --> 00:00:21,040
We select Routine and Investigation.

8
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:25,309
The location and destination remain blank
in this small example; for

9
00:00:25,309 --> 00:00:28,850
your own work, complete the fields your
department requires and use an

10
00:00:28,899 --> 00:00:31,170
accurate, permitted incident location.

11
00:00:31,389 --> 00:00:34,709
Turn on Save as pending, do not dispatch
yet.

12
00:00:35,199 --> 00:00:37,340
Read the explanation beside the switch.

13
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:41,490
The call will be saved and numbered, with
any selected recipients

14
00:00:41,490 --> 00:00:43,460
retained as a proposed dispatch.

15
00:00:43,849 --> 00:00:47,470
Saving a pending record and dispatching it
are separate decisions.

16
00:00:47,829 --> 00:00:52,419
This is useful when a dispatcher needs to
finish a nonurgent request or review

17
00:00:52,419 --> 00:00:54,589
its recipient list before releasing it.

18
00:00:54,759 --> 00:00:59,000
Open Select Recipients and search for
Wallace Boden, our fictional member.

19
00:00:59,439 --> 00:01:02,040
Check his row and leave Everyone
unchecked.

20
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:04,220
The picker shows one selected.

21
00:01:04,659 --> 00:01:07,810
Choose Confirm to return that proposed
selection to the form.

22
00:01:08,259 --> 00:01:12,199
At the bottom, verify one selected, the
pending switch and the

23
00:01:12,199 --> 00:01:14,039
full Save Pending Call button.

24
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:18,480
Check the exact title and description
before choosing Save Pending Call.

25
00:01:18,810 --> 00:01:21,939
The member selected here is still part of
the proposed list.

26
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:24,869
The app opens the pending queue with a
numbered row.

27
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:29,049
Its narrow columns shorten the name and
number, so open the row

28
00:01:29,049 --> 00:01:30,619
to read the complete identity.

29
00:01:31,059 --> 00:01:36,590
This example is F D fifty one, twenty
twenty six, zero zero zero seven.

30
00:01:37,090 --> 00:01:40,319
Call Details says Pending, not yet
dispatched.

31
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:43,989
Open Dispatched and you can already see
Wallace Boden.

32
00:01:44,440 --> 00:01:47,819
While the pending banner remains, that tab
shows the proposed

33
00:01:47,819 --> 00:01:51,770
recipient; its title alone does not mean
the call has been released.

34
00:01:51,959 --> 00:01:55,380
Return to Pending Calls and choose
Dispatch for that record.

35
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:59,200
Review the proposed member in Select
Dispatch Recipients.

36
00:01:59,559 --> 00:02:04,420
Here we clear Boden once: the count
becomes zero and Confirm is disabled.

37
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:09,309
Select him again and verify one selected,
with Everyone still unchecked.

38
00:02:09,690 --> 00:02:12,469
Review this final choice before pressing
Confirm.

39
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:15,960
This is the action that releases the
waiting call to the selected

40
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:20,010
recipient, so use the same care you would
when making a new dispatch.

41
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:22,099
Check the result in two places.

42
00:02:22,470 --> 00:02:25,360
The pending queue now says No pending
calls.

43
00:02:25,699 --> 00:02:30,540
In the same saved call, Dispatched lists
Wallace Boden, with Command as his

44
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:32,779
group and User as the recipient type.

45
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:37,149
The pending banner is gone, and Dispatch
additional resources is available.

46
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:39,159
We leave that separate action alone.

47
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:42,930
These checks confirm the recorded dispatch
state and selected

48
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:44,409
recipient inside the app.

49
00:02:44,619 --> 00:02:49,659
To finish this exercise, check its title
and number, then choose Close Call.

50
00:02:50,179 --> 00:02:53,980
Select Closed under Close Type and enter a
Close Note that

51
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:55,780
identifies the training activity.

52
00:02:56,179 --> 00:02:59,589
Turn off Notify everyone on the call for
this local cleanup.

53
00:03:00,129 --> 00:03:03,670
Read back the type, complete note and
switch before selecting

54
00:03:03,670 --> 00:03:05,310
the final Close Call button.

55
00:03:05,769 --> 00:03:09,689
Close only your exercise record; keep
unrelated incidents and

56
00:03:09,689 --> 00:03:11,189
their command boards intact.

57
00:03:11,319 --> 00:03:14,440
The console returns to three active
training calls, with

58
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:16,299
zero pending and zero scheduled.

59
00:03:16,749 --> 00:03:21,120
Search for our exercise name: the call
panel shows No active calls while

60
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:22,950
the department total remains three.

61
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,200
A warehouse search still finds the
original incident.

62
00:03:26,559 --> 00:03:30,329
Our exercise record also remains readable
in Call Details, with

63
00:03:30,329 --> 00:03:32,140
its Close Call action removed.

64
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:33,880
Clear the search when finished.

65
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:38,429
The same prepare and review pattern can
serve fire and EMS, community

66
00:03:38,429 --> 00:03:42,810
care and mental health teams, search and
rescue, security, wildland

67
00:03:42,810 --> 00:03:45,129
contractors, and industrial teams.

68
00:03:45,469 --> 00:03:50,000
This browser exercise confirms these
application records; it does not establish

69
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,230
notification delivery, a physical response
or native device behavior.

